R = {
"Gutenberg's printing press": ("SCR",3,"Movable metal type made books cheap and turned ideas into something no one could contain.","Johannes Gutenberg combined metal type, oil-based ink and a wine-style press. His Bible came off the press around 1455. Within 50 years, printers in over 250 European towns had produced some 20 million books.","Printing spread the Reformation, the Scientific Revolution and standardized languages.","It was the internet of its age: every later information revolution is compared to it."),
"Ottomans take Constantinople": ("WP",3,"After 1,100 years, the last piece of the Roman Empire fell to Ottoman cannons.","Sultan Mehmed II, just 21, besieged the city with about 80,000 men and huge bronze bombards that pounded the ancient land walls. Constantinople fell on May 29, 1453, and became the Ottoman capital, Istanbul.","With land routes to Asia under Ottoman control, Portugal and Spain looked for sea routes, setting off the Age of Exploration.","The Ottoman Empire lasted until 1922; Istanbul is still Turkey's largest city."),
"Hundred Years' War ends": ("WP",2,"England's long fight for the French throne ended with England losing nearly everything in France.","The war ran on and off from 1337 to 1453. Joan of Arc turned the tide at Orléans in 1429, and French cannons won the final battle at Castillon.","England kept only Calais (until 1558) and turned inward, falling into the Wars of the Roses.","France and England each started seeing themselves as nations, not just kingdoms of a ruling family."),
"Wars of the Roses begin": ("WP",1,"Two branches of England's royal family fought for 30 years over the crown.","The Houses of Lancaster (red rose) and York (white rose) battled after the weak Henry VI lost his grip and nobles took sides.","Henry Tudor won in 1485 and founded the Tudor dynasty.","The wars inspired Shakespeare's history plays and, loosely, 'Game of Thrones'."),
"Ferdinand & Isabella marry": ("P",2,"One wedding joined two kingdoms and created Spain.","Isabella of Castile married Ferdinand of Aragon in secret, against her half-brother's wishes. Together they ruled the two largest Spanish kingdoms as partners.","They finished the Reconquista, launched the Inquisition and funded Columbus, all in 1492.","Their grandson Charles V inherited the first empire on which 'the sun never set'."),
"Spanish Inquisition": ("RP",2,"Spain's monarchs set up a church court to police the faith of new Christians.","Ferdinand and Isabella feared that Jews and Muslims who had converted were secretly keeping their old faiths. The Inquisition investigated, tortured and burned thousands over three centuries.","It enforced religious uniformity and fed the expulsions of 1492 and 1609.","It became a lasting symbol of state-backed religious persecution."),
"Tudors take the English throne": ("PW",2,"Henry Tudor killed Richard III in battle and started England's most famous dynasty.","At Bosworth Field, Richard III was betrayed by his allies and killed. Henry VII married Elizabeth of York, uniting the warring families.","His son Henry VIII and granddaughter Elizabeth I reshaped England.","Richard III's skeleton was found under a parking lot in Leicester in 2012."),
"Granada falls; Jews expelled from Spain": ("RPW",3,"In one year, Spain ended Muslim rule, expelled its Jews and sent Columbus west.","The Emirate of Granada, last Muslim state in Iberia after nearly 800 years, surrendered in January 1492. In March the monarchs ordered Jews to convert or leave; perhaps 100,000 or more left.","Sephardic Jews resettled in the Ottoman Empire, North Africa and the Netherlands.","Spain and Portugal later passed laws offering citizenship to descendants of the expelled Sephardim."),
"Italian Wars begin": ("WP",2,"France invaded Italy and dragged all of Europe's big powers into a 60-year fight.","Charles VIII of France claimed Naples and marched down the peninsula. Spain, the Holy Roman Empire and the Pope all joined in.","Italy was left under Spanish control, but French and Spanish elites carried Renaissance art and ideas home.","Machiavelli wrote 'The Prince' watching Italy become a battleground; modern diplomacy took shape here."),
"Mona Lisa painted": ("C",2,"Leonardo began the portrait that became the world's most famous painting.","Leonardo da Vinci painted Lisa Gherardini, a Florentine merchant's wife, using a soft blending technique called sfumato. He kept working on it for years and never delivered it.","It went to France with him and ended up in the royal collection, then the Louvre.","About 9 million people a year visit the Louvre, many of them mainly to see it."),
"Michelangelo paints Sistine ceiling": ("CR",2,"Michelangelo spent four years painting the story of Genesis on a chapel ceiling.","Pope Julius II hired Michelangelo, who saw himself as a sculptor, to paint the ceiling of the Sistine Chapel. He covered it with over 300 figures, including the Creation of Adam.","It set a new standard for Renaissance art and the papacy's power to impress.","Cardinals still elect popes beneath it."),
"Machiavelli's The Prince": ("IP",2,"A jobless diplomat wrote a handbook on power that shocked readers for centuries.","Exiled from Florence, Niccolò Machiavelli wrote 'The Prince' to win favor with the Medici. He argued rulers should do what works, not what is moral, being 'feared' if they can't be loved.","It was banned by the Church but read by every ruler in Europe.","'Machiavellian' is still a word for ruthless political cunning, and realist foreign policy starts here."),
"Luther's 95 Theses": ("RI",3,"A monk's protest against the Church split Western Christianity in two.","Martin Luther attacked the sale of indulgences, payments that promised to shorten time in purgatory. He argued salvation came by faith alone and the Bible, not the Pope, was the final authority. Printing presses spread his ideas across Germany in weeks.","The Protestant Reformation spread to Switzerland, Scandinavia, Britain and beyond, followed by a century of religious wars.","About 900 million Protestants today; it also fed ideas of individual conscience and literacy."),
"Charles V becomes Holy Roman Emperor": ("P",2,"One man inherited Spain, Austria, the Netherlands and the Americas.","Through a series of royal marriages and deaths, the Habsburg Charles inherited a huge empire, then bought his election as Holy Roman Emperor.","He spent his reign fighting France, the Ottomans and the Protestants, and abdicated exhausted in 1556.","His empire was the first one on which 'the sun never set'."),
"Diet of Worms": ("RP",2,"Luther refused to take back his words before the emperor.","Charles V summoned Luther to an imperial assembly at Worms and ordered him to recant. Luther reportedly said 'Here I stand, I can do no other.' He was declared an outlaw.","A friendly prince hid him in Wartburg Castle, where he translated the New Testament into German.","His German Bible helped standardize the modern German language."),
"Ottomans win at Mohács": ("W",2,"In two hours, the Ottomans destroyed Hungary's army and killed its king.","Suleiman the Magnificent's larger, better-armed force crushed the Hungarians. King Louis II drowned in a river while fleeing.","Hungary was split between Ottomans and Habsburgs for 150 years.","Hungarians still use 'more was lost at Mohács' to mean 'it could be worse'."),
"Ottoman siege of Vienna": ("W",2,"The Ottomans reached the gates of Vienna, then turned back.","Suleiman besieged the Habsburg capital, but rain, supply problems and fierce defense forced him to retreat before winter.","It marked the limit of Ottoman expansion into Central Europe, until 1683.","Vienna became the frontline city between Christian Europe and the Ottoman world."),
"Henry VIII breaks with Rome": ("RP",3,"Henry VIII made himself head of England's church so he could get a divorce.","The Pope refused to annul Henry's marriage to Catherine of Aragon, who had not given him a son. Parliament's Act of Supremacy made the king head of the Church of England, and he closed the monasteries and took their wealth.","England became Protestant, then Catholic, then Protestant again over the next 25 years.","The Church of England and the global Anglican Communion, about 85 million people, come from this."),
"Copernicus: sun-centered universe": ("SI",3,"A Polish astronomer moved the Earth out of the center of the universe.","Nicolaus Copernicus argued that Earth and the planets orbit the Sun. He published only as he was dying, partly fearing ridicule.","Kepler, Galileo and Newton built on his idea over the next 150 years.","A 'Copernican revolution' now means any shift that changes how we see everything."),
"Council of Trent": ("R",2,"The Catholic Church reformed itself and struck back at the Protestants.","Over 18 years, bishops met at Trent to fix corruption, train priests and restate Catholic beliefs the Protestants rejected.","The Counter-Reformation brought new orders like the Jesuits, who spread Catholicism across Asia and the Americas.","Its rules shaped Catholic practice until the 1960s."),
"Peace of Augsburg": ("RP",2,"German princes won the right to pick their state's religion.","After war between Catholic and Lutheran princes, Charles V accepted the rule 'whose realm, his religion'. Calvinists were left out.","The peace lasted until 1618, when it broke into the Thirty Years' War.","It was an early step toward separating religion from imperial politics."),
"Elizabeth I takes throne": ("P",2,"A Protestant queen began a 45-year reign that made England a power.","Elizabeth inherited a divided, nearly bankrupt country after her Catholic half-sister Mary. She never married and used that to keep suitors and rivals guessing.","Her reign saw the Armada's defeat, Shakespeare and the first English colonies.","The 'Elizabethan age' is still seen as England's golden age."),
"French Wars of Religion": ("WR",2,"France tore itself apart for 36 years over Catholic and Protestant faith.","As Calvinism spread among French nobles, the Huguenots and Catholics fought eight wars. Massacres were common on both sides.","Henry IV ended it by converting to Catholicism and granting Huguenots toleration in 1598.","It showed Europe how religious division could destroy a kingdom."),
"Dutch Revolt": ("WPE",2,"The Netherlands rebelled against Spain and created a new kind of country.","Philip II's taxes and persecution of Protestants drove the Dutch to revolt under William of Orange. The war lasted 80 years.","The Dutch Republic became the world's richest trading nation.","The Dutch flag and the Netherlands' tradition of tolerance and commerce come from this fight."),
"Battle of Lepanto": ("W",2,"The largest galley battle in history stopped the Ottoman navy in the West.","A Holy League of Spain, Venice and the Pope met the Ottoman fleet off Greece. About 200 galleys fought on each side; the Ottomans lost most of theirs.","The Ottomans rebuilt fast, but never again dominated the western Mediterranean.","It's the last great battle of oared warships."),
"St. Bartholomew's Day Massacre": ("WR",2,"A royal wedding turned into a slaughter of Protestants.","Huguenot nobles came to Paris for a wedding meant to make peace. Catholic leaders ordered their leaders killed, and mobs murdered thousands across France.","The wars of religion grew even worse.","It's one of Europe's most infamous examples of religious violence."),
"Spanish Armada defeated": ("WP",3,"England survived Spain's great invasion fleet, and its rise at sea began.","Philip II sent 130 ships to escort an army to invade England. English fire ships scattered them at Gravelines, and storms wrecked dozens more off Scotland and Ireland.","Spain remained strong, but England, and the Dutch, grew bolder at sea and in the Americas.","It became a founding myth of English, then British, naval power."),
"Edict of Nantes": ("RP",2,"France's king granted Protestants the right to worship.","Henry IV, a former Protestant who became Catholic to take Paris, granted Huguenots religious freedom and fortified towns.","Louis XIV revoked it in 1685, driving about 200,000 Huguenots abroad.","An early example of a state choosing tolerance to end religious war."),
"English East India Company": ("EP",3,"A group of London merchants got a charter to trade with Asia, and ended up ruling India.","Queen Elizabeth gave the company a monopoly on English trade east of Africa. It started with spices, then shifted to Indian cotton and Chinese tea.","By 1800 it had its own army of about 200,000 and governed much of India.","It's the model of a corporation grown so powerful it became a state."),
"Dutch VOC founded": ("E",3,"The Dutch invented the modern corporation and the stock market.","The VOC (Dutch East India Company) sold shares to the public to fund risky voyages to Asia. Shares traded on the Amsterdam exchange.","It controlled the spice trade, waged wars and colonized Indonesia.","Every publicly traded company today follows its model."),
"Galileo's telescope": ("S",3,"Galileo pointed a telescope at the sky and saw that Copernicus was right.","Galileo improved a Dutch invention and saw Jupiter's moons, the phases of Venus and mountains on the Moon, evidence the Earth isn't the center of everything.","The Church put him on trial in 1633 and confined him to house arrest for life.","Trusting observation over authority is the core of modern science."),
"Thirty Years' War begins": ("WR",3,"Central Europe fought its most destructive war before the 20th century.","It began when Bohemian Protestants threw imperial officials out a castle window in Prague. It became a power struggle among the Habsburgs, Sweden and France fought mostly in Germany.","Armies, famine and plague killed perhaps a third of Germany's population.","The peace that ended it created the modern idea of sovereign states."),
"English Civil War": ("WP",2,"King and Parliament went to war over who really ruled England.","Charles I tried to rule and tax without Parliament and to impose his church on Scotland. Parliament raised an army; Oliver Cromwell's 'New Model Army' won.","Charles was executed in 1649 and England briefly became a republic.","It settled that English monarchs answer to Parliament."),
"Peace of Westphalia": ("PI",3,"The treaties that ended the Thirty Years' War invented the modern state system.","Exhausted powers signed treaties in Münster and Osnabrück. They agreed rulers had sovereignty over their own lands and religion, and other states shouldn't interfere.","The Dutch Republic and Switzerland were recognized as independent; the Holy Roman Empire became much weaker.","'Westphalian sovereignty' is still the foundation of international law."),
"Charles I executed": ("PI",2,"England put its king on trial and cut off his head.","Parliament's army tried Charles I for treason against his own people and beheaded him outside his palace in Whitehall.","England became a republic under Cromwell until 1660.","It was a shocking precedent: kings could be held to account by law."),
"Hobbes' Leviathan": ("I",2,"Written after civil war, Hobbes argued that people need a strong ruler to avoid chaos.","Thomas Hobbes said that without government, life would be 'nasty, brutish, and short'. People agree to give up some freedom to a sovereign for safety.","Locke and Rousseau later built rival versions of this 'social contract'.","It's still the starting point for debates about why we obey governments."),
"Cromwell rules": ("P",1,"England's republic became a military dictatorship.","Oliver Cromwell dissolved Parliament and ruled as Lord Protector. He banned theatres and Christmas celebrations and brutally conquered Ireland.","When he died, the republic collapsed and the monarchy returned.","He's still deeply controversial: a hero of Parliament to some, a war criminal in Ireland."),
"English monarchy restored": ("P",1,"England brought back its king, but on Parliament's terms.","Charles II returned from exile after the republic fell apart. Theatres reopened and the party-going 'Restoration' era began.","Tensions over Catholicism led to the Glorious Revolution in 1688.","England's monarchy has survived since, though with steadily less power."),
"Great Fire of London": ("C",1,"A bakery fire burned most of the City of London.","Starting in Pudding Lane, the fire spread through wooden buildings for four days, destroying 13,000 houses and St Paul's Cathedral.","Christopher Wren rebuilt St Paul's and many churches; new buildings were brick and stone.","It also gave rise to the first fire insurance companies."),
"Ottomans turned back at Vienna": ("W",2,"The Ottomans' second siege of Vienna failed, and their long retreat from Europe began.","A huge Ottoman army besieged Vienna for two months. Polish King Jan Sobieski led a relief army whose cavalry charge broke the siege.","The Habsburgs took Hungary within 16 years.","It's often seen as the high-water mark of Ottoman power in Europe."),
"Newton's Principia": ("S",3,"Newton explained the motion of everything, from apples to planets, with a few laws.","Isaac Newton set out three laws of motion and the law of universal gravitation, showing the same physics works on Earth and in the heavens.","It made the universe predictable and inspired the Enlightenment's faith in reason.","Engineers still use Newton's laws to build bridges and launch rockets."),
"Glorious Revolution": ("PI",3,"England replaced its king almost without bloodshed and made Parliament supreme.","Parliamentary leaders invited the Dutch William of Orange to invade and replace his Catholic father-in-law James II. James fled.","The Bill of Rights limited royal power and laid the basis of constitutional monarchy.","Britain's political system, and the American founders' ideas, rest on this settlement."),
"English Bill of Rights; Locke": ("IP",3,"England limited the king's power, and Locke explained why people may overthrow tyrants.","Parliament's Bill of Rights barred the monarch from taxing or keeping an army without its consent. John Locke argued people have natural rights to life, liberty and property.","Locke's ideas spread across Europe and the colonies.","The Declaration of Independence borrowed Locke almost word for word."),
"Bank of England": ("E",2,"A bank set up to fund a war became the model for central banks.","William III needed money to fight France. Investors lent the government £1.2 million in exchange for a charter as a bank.","Britain could now borrow cheaply, which helped it outfight richer France for a century.","Almost every country now has a central bank built on this model."),
"War of Spanish Succession": ("WP",2,"Europe fought to stop France and Spain from merging under one family.","When the last Spanish Habsburg died, Louis XIV's grandson inherited Spain. Britain, Austria and the Dutch fought to keep the crowns apart.","Britain gained Gibraltar and the right to sell enslaved Africans to Spanish America.","Gibraltar is still British today."),
"Great Britain formed": ("P",2,"England and Scotland joined into one kingdom.","Scotland was nearly bankrupt after a failed colony in Panama; England wanted security against a Scottish alliance with France. The Acts of Union created Great Britain.","Scots played a huge role in the British Empire and the Enlightenment.","Scotland voted to stay in the UK in 2014, but independence is still debated."),
"Battle of Poltava": ("W",2,"Russia crushed Sweden and became a European great power.","Peter the Great defeated Charles XII deep in Ukraine. The Swedish army surrendered days later.","Russia took the Baltic coast and built St. Petersburg as its new capital.","Poltava is still remembered in Russia, Sweden and Ukraine, for very different reasons."),
"Treaty of Utrecht": ("PE",2,"A peace that rebalanced Europe also handed Britain the slave trade to Spanish America.","The treaties ended the War of Spanish Succession. A Bourbon kept Spain, but France and Spain could never unite.","'Balance of power' became the guiding rule of European diplomacy.","Gibraltar, Britain's prize, still sparks disputes with Spain."),
"South Sea Bubble": ("E",2,"Britain's first great stock market crash ruined thousands, including Isaac Newton.","The South Sea Company promised riches from trade with Spanish America. Its shares soared tenfold in months, then collapsed.","Parliament passed laws limiting new companies; Robert Walpole rose to power cleaning up the mess.","Every bubble since, from tulips to dot-coms, gets compared to it."),
"Peter the Great proclaimed emperor": ("P",2,"Peter dragged Russia toward Europe and declared it an empire.","Peter I toured Western Europe in disguise, then forced Russian nobles to shave beards and dress European. He built a navy and a new capital on the Baltic.","Russia became one of Europe's great powers.","Russia still debates whether it is European, Asian or something of its own."),
"Frederick the Great; War of Austrian Succession": ("WP",2,"Prussia's new king seized Austria's richest province.","When Maria Theresa inherited Austria, Frederick II saw weakness and grabbed Silesia. She fought back for decades but never recovered it.","Prussia rose to rival Austria for leadership of Germany.","The rivalry ended only when Prussia unified Germany without Austria in 1871."),
"Lisbon earthquake": ("IS",2,"A giant earthquake on a holy day shook Europe's faith.","On All Saints' Day, an earthquake, tsunami and fires destroyed Lisbon and killed tens of thousands while many were in church.","Thinkers like Voltaire asked how a good God could allow it; Portugal rebuilt with early quake-proof designs.","It's sometimes called the first modern disaster, studied scientifically and debated by philosophers."),
"Seven Years' War": ("W",3,"Britain and France fought a world war across five continents.","Fighting raged in Europe, North America, India, West Africa and on the seas. Britain won Canada and control of India.","Victory left Britain in debt, which led to taxes on its American colonies.","It made Britain the leading global power for 150 years."),
"Catherine the Great; Rousseau's Social Contract": ("PI",2,"A German princess seized Russia's throne the same year Rousseau declared that people are born free.","Catherine overthrew her husband and expanded Russia into Poland and Crimea. Rousseau wrote 'Man is born free, and everywhere he is in chains'.","Rousseau's ideas inspired the French revolutionaries.","Catherine's conquests of Crimea and Ukraine are invoked in Russian politics today."),
"Watt's steam engine": ("SE",3,"James Watt's improved steam engine powered the Industrial Revolution.","Watt added a separate condenser, making engines about four times more efficient. With partner Matthew Boulton he sold them to mines and mills.","Steam drove factories, trains and ships across the world.","Industrialization, and the coal-burning that began climate change, starts here."),
"First Partition of Poland": ("P",2,"Three neighbors carved up Poland like a cake.","Russia, Prussia and Austria seized about a third of Poland. Two more partitions erased it from the map by 1795.","Poland didn't exist as a state for 123 years.","Poles still remember being squeezed between Russia and Germany."),
"Adam Smith's Wealth of Nations": ("EI",3,"A Scottish professor explained how markets make nations rich.","Adam Smith argued that when people pursue their own interest in free markets, an 'invisible hand' benefits everyone. He attacked monopolies and trade barriers.","It became the bible of free trade.","Modern economics starts here; his face is on the British £20 note."),
"French Revolution": ("PIW",3,"France overthrew its king and declared the rights of man.","With France bankrupt and bread too expensive, the Third Estate declared itself a National Assembly. Parisians stormed the Bastille; the Assembly abolished noble privileges and issued the Declaration of the Rights of Man.","It spiraled into the Terror, war across Europe and Napoleon.","Liberty, equality and nationalism, and the terms 'left' and 'right', come from this revolution."),
"Louis XVI executed; the Terror": ("PW",2,"The revolution beheaded its king, then turned on its own people.","Louis XVI was guillotined in January 1793. Robespierre's Committee of Public Safety executed about 17,000 'enemies of the revolution'.","Robespierre himself was guillotined in 1794.","'Terror' entered politics as a word for government by fear."),
"Napoleon seizes power": ("P",2,"A young general took over France in a coup.","Napoleon Bonaparte, famous for victories in Italy, overthrew the weak government and made himself First Consul.","He crowned himself emperor in 1804 and conquered most of Europe.","His legal code still underpins law in France and dozens of other countries."),
"Napoleon crowned emperor": ("PI",2,"Napoleon took the crown from the Pope's hands and put it on his own head.","At Notre-Dame, Napoleon crowned himself to show his power came from himself, not the Church. His Napoleonic Code made all men equal before the law.","His armies spread the Code across Europe.","Countries from Belgium to Louisiana still use laws based on it."),
"Trafalgar & Austerlitz": ("W",2,"In two months, Britain won the seas and Napoleon won the land.","Nelson destroyed the French-Spanish fleet at Trafalgar but was killed. Weeks later Napoleon crushed Austria and Russia at Austerlitz.","Napoleon dominated Europe, but Britain was safe from invasion.","Trafalgar Square in London is named for the battle."),
"Holy Roman Empire ends": ("P",1,"Napoleon ended a 1,000-year-old empire.","After Austerlitz, Napoleon reorganized the German states, and the last emperor dissolved the Holy Roman Empire.","German states merged into fewer, bigger ones, preparing the way for unification.","Voltaire had joked it was neither holy, nor Roman, nor an empire."),
"Britain bans slave trade": ("PE",3,"Britain outlawed the Atlantic slave trade and used its navy to stop it.","After 20 years of campaigning by abolitionists like William Wilberforce and formerly enslaved writers like Olaudah Equiano, Parliament banned the trade.","The Royal Navy's West Africa Squadron freed about 150,000 people from slave ships.","It began the global end of the slave trade, though slavery itself lasted decades more."),
"Napoleon invades Russia": ("W",3,"Napoleon marched 600,000 men into Russia; fewer than 100,000 came out.","Russia had broken Napoleon's trade blockade against Britain. The Russians retreated, burning everything. Napoleon took Moscow, found it empty and burning, and retreated through winter.","Europe united against him; he was exiled in 1814.","'Never invade Russia in winter' became a lesson Hitler ignored in 1941."),
"Waterloo; Congress of Vienna": ("WP",3,"Napoleon's final defeat, and a peace that kept Europe calm for decades.","After escaping exile, Napoleon was defeated by Wellington and Blücher at Waterloo. Diplomats at Vienna redrew Europe to balance power.","The 'Concert of Europe' prevented a general European war for about a century.","'Meeting your Waterloo' still means a final defeat."),
"Greek War of Independence": ("WP",2,"Greece broke free from the Ottomans, inspiring Europe's romantics.","Greek rebels rose up in 1821. European volunteers like Lord Byron joined; Britain, France and Russia sank the Ottoman fleet at Navarino.","Greece became independent in 1830, the first new nation from the Ottoman Empire.","It became a model for other nationalist breakaways in the Balkans."),
"First public steam railway": ("SE",3,"A steam locomotive pulled the first public railway train.","George Stephenson's Locomotion No. 1 ran between Stockton and Darlington, hauling coal and passengers.","Within 25 years Britain had over 6,000 miles of track.","Railways shrank distances and created the modern world of schedules."),
"July Revolution; Belgium independent": ("P",1,"Paris overthrew another king, and Belgium broke from the Netherlands.","Charles X tried to limit voting and press freedom; Parisians rose up and replaced him. A riot after an opera in Brussels sparked Belgium's revolution.","The Vienna settlement started to crack.","Belgium's split between Dutch and French speakers still shapes its politics."),
"Britain abolishes slavery": ("PE",3,"Britain freed about 800,000 enslaved people across its empire.","After huge slave revolts like Jamaica's in 1831 and years of campaigning, Parliament abolished slavery. It paid £20 million, about 40% of the national budget, to slave owners, not to the enslaved.","Former slaves were forced into years of unpaid 'apprenticeship'.","British taxpayers only finished repaying the loan for that compensation in 2015."),
}
